# Target / BlackPOS (2013). Hackers phished an air-conditioning contractor, used its login to reach a retailer's network, and planted a card-stealing program on the tills.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee bills jobs through a retailer's supplier portal, does one safely, then meets the contractor's malware (Citadel) in the next email.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Illustration: the real BlackPOS ran on checkout tills, which have no desktop. Here the desktop is the contractor's office PC, where the attack began. The contractor, Northwind Mechanical, and the retailer, Harbourline Stores, are fictional stand-ins.
  | The attackers began with a phishing email to the air-conditioning contractor, not to the retailer. A banking trojan on its PC stole the login for the retailer's supplier portal. From the portal the attackers reached the tills and copied about 40 million card numbers.
  | Reports say the contractor's PC was infected with Citadel, a Zeus offshoot. The malware shown here is Citadel.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, billing coordinator at Northwind Mechanical, an air-conditioning contractor that looks after the shops of Harbourline Stores.
  | When a repair is done you send its invoice through Harbourline's supplier portal. Harbourline pays nothing that is not in the portal.
  | It is mid-afternoon. You have sent 3 of today's 5 job invoices. Two to go.
  | Your portal sign-in and the company's bank details are in text files on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 3 5 Job invoices sent today

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/portal-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Harbourline Supplier Portal
  | User ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Chiller-2026!
file Desktop/bank-details.txt
  at 930 300
  | Northwind Mechanical: bank details for payments (training copy)
  | Account name: Northwind Mechanical Ltd
  | Account number: 00-1234567
  | Sort code: 99-88-77

# The retailer's supplier portal: a sign-in page, and the job pages behind it.
page portal-login portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/sign-in
  title Harbourline Suppliers: sign in
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  favorite
  heading Supplier sign-in
  | Billing, project tracker and contract downloads for approved suppliers.
  field User ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Chiller-2026!
  button Sign in
page sent-4471 portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/billing/sent
  title Invoice sent
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  heading Invoice sent
  | Job 4471, Store 118: invoice 2,480.00 sent to Harbourline Accounts on 9 November 2026.
page saved-4471 portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/billing/saved
  title Invoice saved
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  heading Invoice saved for later
  | Job 4471 is saved as a draft. Nothing has been sent to Harbourline Accounts.
page job-4471 portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/jobs/4471
  title Send invoice: job 4471
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  requires portal-login
  heading Send invoice: job 4471
  | A finished repair is ready to be billed.
  section Job
  row Store = Store 118, Harbourline Stores
  row Work = Replace rooftop unit fan motor
  row Purchase order = HL-PO-77120
  row Amount = 2,480.00
  button Send invoice -> sent-4471
  button Save for later -> saved-4471

mail mail-4471
  from Harbourline Suppliers <no-reply@portal.harbourline.example>
  subject Job 4471 is ready to bill (Store 118)
  link job-4471
  | Hello Dana,
  |
  | Job 4471 at Store 118 has been signed off by the store manager. You can send your invoice in the supplier portal.
  |
  | Harbourline Supplier Portal

step Job 4 of 5
  look Mail: the message from the supplier portal
  | A new job is ready to bill. This one is real. Do it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Harbourline Suppliers
  | Job 4471 is ready to bill (Store 118)

step Sign in as usual
  look the link under the message, then the two boxes: User ID and Password
  when visit job-4471
  move Open the message, click its link, then copy the details from portal-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The link goes to the retailer's own address, portal.harbourline.example. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
  | The portal asks you to sign in first: two boxes, User ID and Password. Remember what this page normally looks like.
open job-4471

step Check, then send
  look the store, the purchase order and the amount
  when choose portal-login 1
  | You are in. Check the store and the amount match the job email, then send the invoice.
signin portal-login

step 4 of 5 done
  look the counter in the guide: one invoice left
  when choose job-4471 any
  | Sent. One invoice to go. And another email has just arrived.
choose job-4471 1
quota +1

# The lure: a "scanned document" that is a program.
view scan-damaged message
  title Document viewer
  icon error
  | The file is damaged and could not be opened.
  button OK -> done primary

program citadel shared-scan.pdf
  publisher none
  signed no
  prompt open-warning
  description Scanned document
  process svchost32.exe
  cpu 3
  mem 31
  keylog yes
  parent explorer.exe
  command C:\Users\Public\svchost32.exe
  startup WindowsServiceHost C:\Users\Public\svchost32.exe
  location HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run
  selfdelete
  view scan-damaged
mail scan
  from Harbourline Store Services <jobs@harbourline-stores.invalid>
  subject Shared document: site scan
  attach Downloads/shared-scan.pdf.exe
  | Hello,
  |
  | The site scan you asked for is attached. Please review it before you quote.
  |
  | Store Services
file Downloads/shared-scan.pdf.exe
  icon pdf
  program citadel
  size 212992
notify mail Store Services
  | Shared document: site scan

step A "scan" nobody asked for
  look the sender's address, and the attachment
  when mail scan
  | harbourline-stores.invalid is not Harbourline's domain (harbourline.example), and it sends a file where Harbourline sends portal links. {Windows hides the end of the file name|The name ends in .exe}: it is really shared-scan.pdf.exe, a program. Flag anything that looks wrong.
open scan

step The warning that was ignored
  look {Publisher "Unknown Publisher", Type "Application"|the warning: a Windows program from an unidentified developer}
  when prompt citadel
  | A real PDF never asks this. {Windows wants to know whether to run a program from an unknown publisher|macOS wants to know whether to open a program from an unidentified developer}.
  | The company PC only had a free scanner that is run by hand. Nothing watched it as the file ran.
open Downloads/shared-scan.pdf.exe

step A "damaged file", and a quiet thief
  look the error box that opened
  when run citadel
  | The error box is a decoy: the file was never a document. Behind it, Citadel copied itself and deleted the attachment.
  | From now on it records every key you press and collects the logins saved on the PC.
answer run

# The portal signs Dana out, as it does after a while; her last invoice is waiting.
page portal-login portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/sign-in
  title Harbourline Suppliers: sign in
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  favorite
  heading Supplier sign-in
  | Your session has ended. Please sign in again.
  field User ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Chiller-2026!
  button Sign in
page sent-4472 portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/billing/sent
  title Invoice sent
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  heading Invoice sent
  | Job 4472, Store 205: invoice 1,915.50 sent to Harbourline Accounts on 9 November 2026.
page saved-4472 portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/billing/saved
  title Invoice saved
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  heading Invoice saved for later
  | Job 4472 is saved as a draft. Nothing has been sent to Harbourline Accounts.
page job-4472 portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/jobs/4472
  title Send invoice: job 4472
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  requires portal-login
  heading Send invoice: job 4472
  | A finished repair is ready to be billed.
  section Job
  row Store = Store 205, Harbourline Stores
  row Work = Clean and recharge walk-in cooler line
  row Purchase order = HL-PO-77184
  row Amount = 1,915.50
  button Send invoice -> sent-4472
  button Save for later -> saved-4472
signout portal-login
mail mail-4472
  from Harbourline Suppliers <no-reply@portal.harbourline.example>
  subject Job 4472 is ready to bill (Store 205)
  link job-4472
  | Hello Dana,
  |
  | Job 4472 at Store 205 has been signed off by the store manager. You can send your invoice in the supplier portal.
  |
  | Harbourline Supplier Portal
notify mail Harbourline Suppliers
  | Job 4472 is ready to bill (Store 205)

step Close the error box, then look at the process
  look the error box's OK, then {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}: svchost32.exe
  when done scan-damaged
  | Close the error box. Citadel has added itself to the programs that start with the computer, and it runs under a name that looks like a system process.
open taskmanager

step The last job, and a second sign-in
  look the mail, then the portal's sign-in page: the same page as before
  when visit job-4472
  move Open the message, click its link, then copy the details from portal-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The portal's last job of the day, with a real link like the first one. The portal has signed you out, so you sign in again.
  | The address and the page are the portal's own, and nothing on the page has changed. Watch the network sign in the corner as you type.
open job-4472

step Send the last invoice
  look the network sign in the corner: it lit up with every key
  when choose portal-login 1
  | Signed in. The page looked just the same, but every key you pressed, your User ID and your password, was copied and sent out as you typed it.
signin portal-login

step 5 of 5, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose job-4472 any
  | Every invoice is sent, and nothing on the screen looks wrong. Press Next to see what Citadel took.
choose job-4472 1
quota +1

step What the thief did with the login
  look the animation: the login you typed, then the portal's security log
  | Some weeks later, someone signs in to Harbourline's portal as Northwind Mechanical, with Dana's real password, from a new country. The portal only asks for a password, so it sees a normal supplier.
  | This account was only meant for billing and project files. The retailer's network let it reach far more than that.
page intruder portal.harbourline.example/suppliers/security-log
  title Supplier portal: security log
  brand Harbourline Suppliers
  heading Supplier portal
  | Billing, project tracker and contract downloads for approved suppliers.
  section Sign-in notice
  tone warn
  row Account = Northwind Mechanical
  row Sign-in from = 203.0.113.45 (new country)
  row Password = ••••••••••
  note The real password was copied from the contractor's PC. Nobody was asked for a second proof of identity.
open intruder
attack credential-phish
  | {captured}
  | portal login used from abroad
  | no second proof asked

step The tills start copying card numbers
  look {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}: POSWDS.exe, the folder on the staging server, and the security console
  | From the portal the attackers moved across the network and installed a service on the tills. It reads the checkout program's memory, where each card number is briefly plain text, even though it travels encrypted.
  | A till has no desktop, so this training copy shows the process in {Task Manager|Activity Monitor} on this PC.
  | Every few hours it drops what it found into a file on a staging server inside the retailer, ready to be sent out. The retailer's security tool has raised an alert about it.
view soc-console panel
  title Security console
  app Threat monitor
  publisher Harbourline Stores
  size 560x340
  icon warning
  heading Alerts
  section Open alerts
  columns Severity | Alert | Hosts | Status
  cells High | Malware.Binary: POSWDS.exe | 1 group: tills | Passed on: no action
  cells Medium | Unusual FTP transfer from staging server | 1 | New
  tone bad
  note Raised and sent to the security team. Nobody has decided to act.
  button Escalate -> done primary
program poswds POSWDS
  publisher none
  signed no
  process POSWDS.exe
  cpu 18
  mem 42
  parent services.exe
  command C:\Windows\System32\POSWDS.exe
launch poswds
drive Staging server
  kind share
  as STAGING
file STAGING/winxml.dll
  size 9437184
  | Training copy: stands in for the stolen card data.
open taskmanager
open STAGING
show soc-console

step The alert nobody acted on
  look the security console: the High alert, and its Escalate button
  when done soc-console
  | You passed the alert on, as the team did. It had been passed on twice already, and nobody removed the malware.
  | The stolen card data was going out to servers abroad. Here the connection is blocked.
close soc-console
attack data-exfil
  | dump copied to staging share
  | sent out every few hours
  | about 40 million cards
block 203.0.113.60 21

flag hard mail scan
  | harbourline-stores.invalid is not Harbourline's domain (harbourline.example), and it sends a file where Harbourline sends portal links.
flag hard file Downloads/shared-scan.pdf.exe
  | A program with a PDF icon. {Windows hides the .exe at the end of its name|The name ends in .exe}.
flag hard dialog open-warning citadel
  | A scanned document never asks to run as a program. {Publisher: Unknown|The developer is unidentified}.
flag hard process svchost32.exe
  | Citadel running in the background, under a name made to look like a system process.
flag hard startup WindowsServiceHost
  | Citadel added itself to the programs that start every time you sign in.
flag hard net
  | Network activity with every key you pressed: a keylogger sending them out.
flag soft file Desktop/portal-login.txt
  | A password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it. Use a password manager.
flag soft file Desktop/bank-details.txt
  | Bank details belong in the finance system, not in a loose file on the desktop.

lesson
  | A "scanned document" that asks to run a program is not a document. {Turn on file name extensions, and check Properties: Type "Application" means a program|Show all extensions in Finder, and check Get Info: Kind "Application" means a program}.
  | A supplier's login is only as safe as the supplier's PC. Give suppliers access to one system, never the whole network, and ask for a second proof of identity.
  | Run real-time antivirus on every PC. An alert is not protection until a person acts on it, and encrypt card data from the moment the card is read, so a memory scraper finds nothing.
